    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. You mentioned \"systemic friction\" in the context of signalized intersections—can you expand on that a bit? I'm specifically interested in what causes those unpredictable moments of friction or sudden slowdowns that don't seem to follow the standard cycle of the lights. What's actually driving that disruption in the flow?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Systemic friction at signalized intersections primarily arises from short-term fluctuations that exacerbate during high traffic volumes or peak hours. Factors like traffic incidents or accidents, which obstruct lanes, and vehicles breaking suddenly can introduce moments of unpredictable friction. Pedestrian crossings that are not timed well with approaching vehicles can also contribute to delays. To add, poorly calibrated signal timings may lead to frequent stopping and starting, which cumulatively manifest as friction in traffic flow. Finally, queuing dynamics—such as vehicles bunching up due to turning behavior or misjudgments of gaps—can result in systemic friction that isn't directly related to the signal cycle itself. Countermeasures include better signal timing optimization, increased awareness campaigns, and promoting alternate routes to manage demand.",
      "source": "target",
